The 6-Step End-to-End Flow (From Architecture Diagram)
Product checks user on Wallet: A customer applies for a new product (e.g., credit card).
Wallet checks KYC on Blockchain: The backend queries the Canton Ledger to see if an active KycRecord contract exists.
3a. If Active → Share Digital ID: The backend uses the existing record and shares the status with the new product.
3b. If Not Present/Expired → Initiate Mitek: The backend asks the user to scan documents (via the webcam UI), sends them to Mitek for verification, and stores them in S3.
Result on Blockchain + Docs in Storage: Upon Mitek approval, a hash of the document is stored in MinIO, and a new KycRecord is minted on Canton.
Wallet shares KYC status: The API returns the verified status and Canton contract ID to the product.
ReKYC Triggered: A scheduled Quartz job monitors expiry dates and triggers step 3b again automatically.
2. Backend Code Architecture (Layer-by-Layer)
The Spring Boot application is structured into standard decoupled layers to ensure separation of concerns, testability, and maintainability.

Layer 1: Controllers (REST API Entry Point)
KycController.java: Exposes POST /api/v1/kyc/initiate. It accepts multipart/form-data containing a JSON request (Customer ID, DID) and a file (the scanned document). It delegates immediately to the Orchestration service.
Layer 2: Orchestration (The Brain)
KycOrchestrationService.java: This is the most critical class. It implements the 6-step flow. It doesn't contain business logic itself; rather, it coordinates transactions across multiple external and internal services (Canton, Mitek, S3, DB, Audit).
Layer 3: Integration Services (External Gateways)
CantonLedgerService.java: Handles all gRPC calls to the Canton participant node. In a production environment, this uses the DamlLedgerClient to exercise DAML choices (like Create KycRecord).
MitekIntegrationService.java: REST client (using RestClient or WebClient) that handles OAuth2 authentication with Mitek, creates dossiers, uploads evidence, and polls for AI verification results.
DocumentStorageService.java: Uses AWS SDK to interact with S3/MinIO. Handles AES-256 encryption before writing to the bucket and computes SHA-256 hashes.
Layer 4: Data Access (Persistence)
JPA Repositories & Entities: While Canton is the source of truth for immutable records, PostgreSQL is used as a read-model cache and for tracking operational data (e.g., Mitek dossier IDs, local file references, audit logs for fast querying).
Flyway Migration (V1__initial_schema.sql): Ensures the database schema is version-controlled and created automatically on startup.
3. Deep Dive: The KycOrchestrationService Code Flow
Here is exactly how the code maps to the architecture diagram when the user clicks "Submit for KYC" on the frontend:

java

@Transactional
public KycRecordEntity initiateKyc(KycInitiateRequest request, MultipartFile document) {
    String customerId = request.getCustomerId();

    // STEP 2: Wallet checks KYC status on Blockchain (via DB cache synced with Canton)
    KycRecordEntity existingKyc = kycRecordRepository.findByCustomerId(customerId).orElse(null);

    if (existingKyc != null && existingKyc.getStatus() == KycStatus.ACTIVE) {
        // STEP 3a: KYC Active -> Reuse Identity
        auditService.logEvent("KYC_INITIATED", customerId, "Existing Active KYC found. Sharing status.");
        return existingKyc; // Short-circuits the flow, saving time and money
    }

    // STEP 3b: If KYC Not Present/Expired -> Initiate KYC via Mitek
    auditService.logEvent("KYC_INITIATED", customerId, "Initiating new KYC via Mitek.");
    String dossierId = mitekIntegrationService.createDossier(customerId);

    // STEP 4a: Docs in Secure Storage (Off-chain)
    String storageRef = documentStorageService.storeDocument(customerId, document);
    String docHash = documentStorageService.computeHash(document);

    // STEP 4b: Mitek AI Verification
    boolean verified = mitekIntegrationService.submitDocuments(dossierId, document);

    // Prepare local entity
    KycRecordEntity kycRecord = new KycRecordEntity();
    kycRecord.setId(UUID.randomUUID());
    kycRecord.setCustomerId(customerId);
    kycRecord.setMitekDossierId(dossierId);
    kycRecord.setVerifiedAt(LocalDateTime.now());
    kycRecord.setExpiresAt(LocalDateTime.now().plusYears(1));

    if (verified) {
        // STEP 4c: KYC Result stored on Blockchain (On-chain)
        // We only store the HASH, never the PII document itself
        String cantonContractId = cantonLedgerService.createKycRecord(customerId, docHash);
        kycRecord.setCantonContractId(cantonContractId);
        kycRecord.setStatus(KycStatus.ACTIVE);
    } else {
        kycRecord.setStatus(KycStatus.REJECTED);
    }

    // Save to local DB for fast querying next time
    kycRecordRepository.save(kycRecord);

    // Immutable Audit Log
    auditService.logEvent("KYC_COMPLETED", customerId, "Status: " + kycRecord.getStatus());

    // STEP 5: Wallet shares KYC status/ID with Product (Returned in HTTP Response)
    return kycRecord;
}
4. DAML Smart Contracts (Canton Ledger)
The reason we use Canton (DAML) instead of a standard database is multi-party trust. Partner banks can trust the KYC status without needing to see the physical documents, and regulators can audit the consent trail without exposing PII.

KycRecord.daml: This template represents the verified identity.
Signatories: Requires both the kycProvider (Lloyds) and the customer to agree to the contract.
Data: Stores did, status, verificationLevel, and documentHashList.
Choices: Includes ExpireKyc (callable only by the bank) and ShareWithParty (callable only by the customer, implementing consent-based sharing).
ConsentRecord.daml: Ensures that a partner bank cannot view a customer's KYC status unless the customer exercises a choice to grant them access. This makes audits legally sound.
5. ReKYC Lifecycle Engine (Step 6)
In a full production system, the ReKycSchedulerService utilizes Spring's @Scheduled annotation or Quartz:

Every night at 2 AM, it queries the database for kyc_records where expiresAt is within the next 30 days.
It triggers a notification to the customer via NotificationService ("Your KYC is expiring soon").
If the customer doesn't respond, on the expiry date, it calls cantonLedgerService.expireKycRecord() to update the blockchain, and updates the local DB status to EXPIRED, forcing step 3b to occur next time the user applies for a product.
